They serve different purposes:
- requested_application_id records which app the caller targeted—even if the ID is unknown or invalid.
- application_id is a foreign key to an existing app. It is NULL when the requested app cannot be resolved.
Example:
Request target	requested_application_id	application_id
Existing app	Requested UUID	Same UUID
Unknown app	Requested UUID	NULL
Malformed ID	INVALID_APPLICATION_ID	NULL


This preserves rejected-request tracking without breaking the foreign-key constraint.

Table	Fields	Verdict	Why
incoming_requests	requested_application_id + application_id	Keep	Original input versus successfully resolved FK. Preserves malformed and unknown IDs for audit.
received_events	event_type_id + event_type	Keep	FK to the event type versus immutable routing-name snapshot.
delivery_projections	endpoint_id + endpoint_url	Keep	Endpoint identity versus destination URL at delivery creation. Historical deliveries must not change when the endpoint URL changes.
incoming_requests	api_key_id + api_key_name	Keep	Key identity versus historical display name. Renaming a key should not rewrite old request history.
audit_logs	application_id + client_id	Keep with rationale	Application scope versus mandatory company scope. client_id is derivable for app-level logs, but useful for company-level logs where application_id is NULL.
workspace_members	client_id + user_id + application_id	Keep	client_id is technically derivable, but supports composite foreign keys that prevent cross-company mappings.
delivery_projections	attempt_count + attempt rows	Keep	Summary value for frequent dashboard reads versus detailed attempt history.
delivery_projections	http_status / last_error + attempt history	Keep, conditionally	Useful denormalized latest-attempt summary. Avoid joining the attempt table for every delivery-list query.
received_events	delivery_count + delivery records	Keep with clearer name	It records the initial active destination count, not necessarily the current number of deliveries after replays.

